;;; `complete()` writes into the fork it is asked about — the record site every
;;; corpus solve uses. Recorded, not repaired (M1e D3, banked 2026-08-28).
;;
;; `record_node` at solve.rs:1977, guarded by `complete()` at :1895.
;;
;; The comment above that guard says consistency is already established because
;; `result.kb` is "that unmutated fork". `complete()` then mutates it:
;; generation runs the full filter pipeline, and the lookahead kill cache
;; writes `(not h)` into the very KB the next lines record.
;;
;; So the engine records `S ∪ K`, where K is what the cache wrote, and checked
;; the criteria against `S`. Here a rule reads K.
;;
;;   default : k=2, model 1 = {(q A), (not (p A)), (not (p B))} — re-saturates
;;             to Contradiction
;;   -K      : k=2, models {(q A)} and {(p A),(p B)} — both correct
;;
;; `k` is 2 either way, so nothing in the read-out signals the difference.
;;
;; ── Why this file carries no `:expect` ────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; Because none can be written for it. Its two models populate **different**
;; relations — `{(q A)}` and `{(p A), (p B)}` — and every arm of an
;; `(or (model …) …)` must name the goal's relations, so whichever relation is
;; the goal, one arm cannot name it and the file does not load. Nor would an
;; `(or …)` detect the fix: a `k = 1` answer still matches one of its arms.
;;
;; So this fixture is pinned by its exit code and its KB-shape digest and by
;; nothing that states an answer, which is Q-M1e.13 — and it is the reason the
;; two `alive-empty-*` files moved their goal to `is-a` rather than following
;; this one.

(relation is-a T T)
(relation marker)
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(marker)
(is-a A T) (is-a B T)

(hrule guess-q () :match (marker)    :assert (q A))
(hrule guess-p () :match (is-a ?x T) :assert (p ?x))

;; One step from any (p ?x) once (q A) holds, so inside the fork that committed
;; to (q A) the lookahead kills every p candidate and caches both negatives.
(rule kill-p ()
  :match  (and (q A) (p ?x))
  :assert (false)
  :priority 300)

;; Reads stored negatives only — no `absent`, so no NAF boundary is in the
;; argument. Dormant at root and in any fork holding a p fact.
(rule totality ()
  :match  (and (not (p A)) (not (p B)))
  :assert (false)
  :priority 400)

(query :goal (q ?x))
